{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>He shall not multiply for himself horses</b> — but only enough for his chariot, so that he does not bring the people back to Egypt, for horses come from there, as it is said: \"And a chariot came up and went out from Egypt for six hundred silver\" (I Kings 10:29). This is the language of Rashi. And it was difficult for me in this matter, for they said in the Jerusalem Talmudi at the end of Sanhedrin (end of chapter 10): \"For resettlement you do not return, but you may return for trade, for commerce, and to conquer the land.\" And if the king sends and purchases from there the horses and the chariots, this is trade and is permitted. And it is possible that the intent of Scripture is that when it warned \"he shall not multiply for himself horses,\" this applies even from his own land and from the land of Shinar, or by way of permitted trade — that he not trust in his chariot because it is great, and in his horsemen because they are very mighty, but let his trust be in the name of his Elohim. And afterwards Scripture warned the king not to bring the people back to Egypt, that he not have there, from his servants and from his people, officers of acquisition dwelling in the chariot-cities for the purpose of multiplying horses, as it is said concerning Solomon: \"And all the cities of the store-cities that Solomon had, and the chariot-cities, etc.\" (I Kings 9:19). And so he had them in Egypt, as it says: \"And the export of horses that Solomon had was from Egypt, and the guild of the king's merchants, etc.\" (I Kings 10:28) — for no man was permitted to export horses from Egypt without the permission of the king of Egypt, and they would give him a tax from them. And Pharaoh gave Solomon the export of horses, meaning that he would export as he wished, and that he would grant permission to whomsoever he desired, and the tax would be his. And the merchants he had there would stand in Egypt purchasing all the horses and sending them to their master as they wished, and selling the others to the rest of the kings of the earth. And that is what it says: \"And likewise to all the kings of the Hittites and to all the kings of Aram, by their hand they would export\" (I Kings 10:29), meaning: by the hand of Solomon's merchants they would export them, and to him they would give the tax." "And the meaning of <b>רַק (only)</b> is to say: even though you shall set over yourself a king like all the nations that are around you — \"only\" let him not be like their kings; he shall not multiply horses as they do, for the entire desire of those kings is to multiply for themselves horses and horsemen." "And the meaning of <b>and HaShem said to you</b> is that HaShem says to you that you shall not again return by the way of Egypt. And similarly: \"as YHWH your Elohim has commanded you\" (Deuteronomy 5:16) — meaning that He commands you thus. Or <b>אָמַר לָכֶם (He said to you)</b> — that He said to me thus, to command you, in the manner of: \"And Moses said, 'Thus says YHWH: About midnight,' etc.\" (Exodus 11:4). And according to the way of our Sages (Yerushalmi Sukkah 5:1): \"For as you have seen Egypt today, you shall never see them again\" (Exodus 14:13) — this is a commandment. And Moses mentioned \"and HaShem said to you that you shall not again return by this way anymore,\" and I have already explained it there. And the reason for this commandment is that the Egyptians and the Canaanites were evil and very sinful before HaShem, as it says: \"After the doings of the land of Egypt, wherein you dwelt, you shall not do; and after the doings of the land of Canaan, etc.\" (Leviticus 18:3). And HaShem desired that Israel not learn from their deeds, and He cut off from the Canaanites every living soul (Deuteronomy 20:16), and He said: \"They shall not dwell in your land\" (Exodus 23:33), and He warned concerning Egypt that we not dwell there in their land."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the Yerushalmi at the end of Sanhedrin; the specific chapter reference (end of ch. 10) is standard for this passage in the Yerushalmi Sanhedrin."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:43.066413435Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}